Sure! In Java you can walk the DOM tree starting at your `SAMLAssertionNode` (which is a subclass of `org.w3c.dom.Node`) and collect all child nodes that are instances of `org.w3c.dom.Element`. Here’s a quick, reusable snippet that does exactly that:

```java
import org.w3c.dom.*;

public class XmlUtils {

    /**
     * Returns a List containing all child nodes of {@code parent} that are
     * instances of {@link Element}.  The order of the returned list matches
     * the document order of the children.
     *
     * @param parent the node whose children you want to inspect
     * @return a List<Element> – never null, but may be empty
     */
    public static List<Element> getChildElements(Node parent) {
        List<Element> result = new ArrayList<>();

        if (parent == null) {
            return result;   // defensive: avoid NPE
        }

        NodeList children = parent.getChildNodes();
        for (int i = 0; i < children.getLength(); i++) {
            Node child = children.item(i);
            if (child instanceof Element) {
                result.add((Element) child);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

### How to use it

```java
// Assume you already have a parsed Document and have located your SAMLAssertionNode:
Document doc = ...;                     // obtained via DocumentBuilder.parse(...)
Node samlAssertionNode = ...;           // e.g., doc.getDocumentElement() or a specific lookup

List<Element> childElements = XmlUtils.getChildElements(samlAssertionNode);

for (Element el : childElements) {
    System.out.println("Tag name: " + el.getTagName());
    // You can now work with each Element (e.g., read attributes, text content, etc.)
}
```

### Why this works

* `Node.getChildNodes()` returns a `NodeList` that contains **all** child nodes, including text nodes, comments, processing instructions, etc.
* By checking `instanceof Element`, we filter out everything that isn’t an element node.
* The method returns an empty list instead of `null` so callers don’t have to perform a null‑check.

### Things to keep in mind

1. **Namespace awareness** – If you’re working with namespaced XML (as SAML usually is), you’ll want to make sure your `DocumentBuilderFactory` was created with `setNamespaceAware(true)` so that `getTagName()` returns the fully qualified name (`{namespaceURI}localName`).  
2. **Performance** – For very large documents, consider using a streaming API (StAX) or a SAX parser if you only need to process a few elements.  
3. **Thread safety** – DOM objects are not thread‑safe. If you plan to use the resulting list across threads, copy the data into immutable structures first.

That should give you a clean, reusable way to pull out all child `<Element>` nodes from any `Node` (including your `SAMLAssertionNode`). Happy coding!
